MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: dialogue_table_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 57db7eafe86a3fd4ca58917620bd8abe, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 57855467520
    m_Localized: "This is the Carondelet Palace. It has stood here for centuries,
      housing the collective memory of the Republic of Ecuador. Are you wondering
      why my name graces its stone? Feel free to rotate the building and explore
      the interactive points to learn more about this landmark. Or, if you\u2019re
      ready to step inside, simply click 'Enter'."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 57956130816
    m_Localized: "We have rolled out the red carpet for your visit. Everything is
      prepared for you to explore the history of one of Ecuador\u2019s most significant
      landmarks. As far back as the early 1600s, the Real Audiencia was already weaving
      the fate of the Spanish Crown within these bounds, and much has unfolded since.
      Now, the past is waiting for you to uncover it."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6482695831183360
    m_Localized: Greetings to all! I am the Baron of Carondelet. Welcome to this
      virtual experience, designed to preserve, share and promote the cultural, historical,
      and political heritage of Ecuador. I shall be your humble host as we walk the
      hallowed halls of my namesake Palace.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691447484416
    m_Localized: "You are about to enter the main courtyard. Ahead, the grand staircase
      leads you to salute the National Flag. In the North Wing, you can visit the
      fountain, while the South Wing houses the Exhibition Hall. The palace, as you
      see it today, was extensively remodeled between 1956 and 1960, during the administration
      of Camilo Ponce Enr\xEDquez."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204672
    m_Localized: "You have reached one of the most pivotal points of the palace and
      this tour. To continue, it is tradition to bow to the National Flag. This gesture
      honors Ecuador\u2019s national symbols and this sanctuary of the country\u2019s
      heritage. You may now continue your journey."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204673
    m_Localized: "In 1960, during the palace\u2019s reconstruction, Oswaldo Guayasam\xEDn\u2014one
      of Ecuador\u2019s most renowned 20th-century artists\u2014was commissioned
      to create a mural. It was designed to be viewed from both the first and second
      floors, offering a truly unique perspective. Step closer to the interactive
      point:  the art has a story to tell."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204674
    m_Localized: Splendid! We have reached the second floor, where you can continue
      navigating through history. Here lies the Presidential Office, the Cabinet
      Room, the Yellow Hall, the Great Banquet Hall, the oratory and the terrace.
      Follow the corridors and look for the signs to find your way. Onward, then!
      We have much ground to cover.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204675
    m_Localized: "Welcome to the Presidential Office\u2014the most important office
      in the nation. Whenever the President is in session here, the Tarqui Grenadiers
      stand as silent sentinels at the door. Inside, you can explore portraits and
      sculptures of the illustrious figures who inspire those who lead this country.
      Take a closer look to learn their stories."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204676
    m_Localized: This is the Cabinet Room, where the President meets with ministers,
      secretaries, and advisors. Explore the room to see the official seating arrangements
      and discover the fine details of the space. Feel free to interact with the
      elements here, or return to the corridor to visit another hall.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204677
    m_Localized: "A new space to explore: the famous Hall of Presidents. Known to
      many as the 'Yellow Room' for the lustrous French tapestries that adorn its
      walls, it has been the stage for our most vital ceremonies since 1960. This
      is the setting for official protocols, such as ministerial appointments and
      the signing of decrees. You will also find portraits of Ecuador\u2019s past
      presidents here\u2014look for the icon to view them."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204678
    m_Localized: "A magnificent space, is it not? The Great Banquet Hall. It is the
      theatre of our social life\u2014receptions, foreign delegations, and grand
      assemblies. Since its 1960 renovation, it has housed everything from historic
      portraiture to porcelain from ages past. When you have satisfied your curiosity,
      seek out the oratory at the far end."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204679
    m_Localized: "In the mid-90s, under Sixto Dur\xE1n Ball\xE9n, this space was
      transformed from a smoking room into a sanctuary of peace. It was born from
      a need for quiet reflection during the Cenepa War. Today, it guards the religious
      relics of the Palace's heritage. Take a moment to interact with these sacred
      images."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691506204680
    m_Localized: "The terrace offers a commanding view of Independence Square\u2014the
      heart of the city and the nation\u2019s civic life. Surrounding it yopu may
      see  the Primada Cathedral, the City Hall, and the Archbishop's Palace. Above
      us on the fa\xE7ade, you may find the National Flag, the bell tower, and the
      clock that marks our history. When you are ready, you may return to the corridor
      or go back to the ground floor."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6465691510398976
    m_Localized: "This is the Carondelet Palace Exhibition Hall. It hosts both permanent
      and temporary exhibits that offer a deeper understanding of Ecuador's journey.
      Immerse yourself in the nation\u2019s memory and learn about our Republican
      era and the heroic Battle of Pichincha."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 8690804431544320
    m_Localized: "As it is the custom for every guest of the Palace, we ask that
      you take a moment to bow to the National Flag. This is a sign of respect for
      Ecuador\u2019s national symbols and the historical heritage preserved here.
      You may now move forward."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 9352858419093504
    m_Localized: 'Our temporary exhibit, ''Days of the Homeland,'' marks two centuries
      since the victory at Pichincha. Drawing from historical documents, it traces
      the path from the independence movement to the centennial celebrations. The
      title refers to two defining dates: August 10, 1809, and May 24, 1822. Learn
      more about these turning points, or return to the main courtyard.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 86150755235930112
    m_Localized: "This is the Blue Room, a space remodeled during the administration
      of President Daniel Noboa Az\xEDn to meet modern institutional communication
      needs. It serves as the hub for press conferences and high-level briefings,
      acting as a strategic point for official government messages. Here, the Carondelet
      Spokesperson and other officials address the nation, making it a vital stage
      for dialogue between the Presidency, the media, and the public."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
